summary:
A bug has been identified in the code of the PaprController.sol, which can be found at https://github.com/with-backed/papr/blob/9528f2711ff0c1522076b9f93fba13f88d5bd5e6/src/PaprController.sol#L486. This bug can be exploited by an attacker to prevent users from interacting with some of the protocol functions. In particular, the attacker can apply a grieving attack by preventing users from reducing their debt or buying and reducing their debt in one transaction.

To demonstrate how this attack works, consider the example of Alice, an honest user who has a debt of 1000 PaprToken and intends to repay her debt fully. Alice calls the reduceDebt function with the correct parameters, but Bob, a malicious user who owns a small amount of PaprToken, notices Alice's transaction in the Mempool and applies a front-run attack. Bob calls the reduceDebt function with Alice's address, the NFT used as collateral, and an amount of 1. By doing so, Bob repays only 1 PaprToken on behalf of Alice, leaving her debt at 1000 * 10**18 - 1. When Alice's transaction is executed, it fails due to an underflow error since Alice's debt is now lower than the amount she was trying to repay.

The attacker can apply this attack for all other users who are trying to repay their debt fully, as it is very cheap to do so. This attack can be applied on transactions that are interacting with the function _reduceDebt, which includes the functions buyAndReduceDebt and reduceDebt. As a result, the attacker can prevent users from calling these functions.

